# Normalize line endings in the repository to LF regardless of what OS a
# contributor develops on. Without this, a Windows checkout with git's
# autocrlf=true converts every text file to CRLF on disk; if that gets
# committed back, later additions like a shell script or Dockerfile
# COPY'd file with a shebang line can fail with "bad interpreter: no such
# file or directory" inside a Linux container, since the kernel reads the
# CRLF as part of the interpreter path. Forcing LF in the repo itself
# means every contributor's checkout is byte-identical no matter their
# local git config or OS.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files, if any are ever added, genuinely need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary files — never let git attempt line-ending conversion on these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.pdf binary
